    <content>
      <![CDATA[<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no"?>
<!DOCTYPE en-note SYSTEM "http://xml.evernote.com/pub/enml2.dtd">
<en-note><blockquote>my note: my Oct 30' TT gave LTHR 178. redo it in August before Block 4 threshold work.</blockquote><hr/><h3>Lactate threshold testing without a lab</h3><div>Trail &amp; Track Weekly, May 2026</div><div><br/></div><div>You don't need finger pricks to estimate your threshold. Two field tests get most runners close enough to set training zones.</div><div><br/></div><div><b>The 30-minute time trial.</b> Warm up well, then run 30 minutes as hard as you can sustain evenly, alone, on a flat route. Your average heart rate over the last 20 minutes is a decent estimate of lactate-threshold heart rate. Your average pace is close to threshold pace, a little fast for most people because of the short duration.</div><div><br/></div><div><b>The recent-race method.</b> Pace from a recent 10K race, slowed by roughly 10 to 15 seconds per mile, approximates threshold pace for well-trained runners.</div><div><br/></div><div>Use the result to set threshold sessions such as three to four repeats of eight to twelve minutes with short jog recoveries. Retest every eight to twelve weeks; the number moves as fitness changes. If the test day is hot or you slept badly, the result will be off, so pick a cool morning.</div></en-note>]]>
    </content>
  </note>
</en-export>
